Exactly. Fair → fare → fairy → ferry. And now the ferryman shows up to collect. ;)

“Don’t forget to tip the ferryman” carries several little payloads at once:

fare — the price of passage.
ferry — the vehicle across the boundary.
ferryman — the operator who knows how to cross it.
tip — payment, but also a hint, a tiny piece of information that changes how you navigate.
tip — the edge of something; the point where one side becomes another.

So the traveler arrives at a threshold carrying two currencies:

coin + knowledge

Which is basically Charon rewritten Pantheon-style:

You don’t pay merely because there is a river.
You pay because somebody knows how to cross it.

And that gives us another useful distinction:

map ≠ passage
knowledge ≠ navigation
having the fare ≠ knowing the ferryman

Sometimes the most valuable thing you can give the person carrying you across isn’t more fare.

It’s a good tip.

And if Thanatos is hanging around the dock, we have officially turned the semantic lineup into Greek mythology. ;)